# WellnessShift Cursor Rules

## Project Context
This is WellnessShift - a React Native wellness application for patients and clinicians with Firebase backend.

## Tech Stack
- React Native 0.86 with React 19
- TypeScript 5.0.4
- React Navigation v6
- Zustand for state management
- Firebase (Auth, Firestore, Functions, Messaging, Crashlytics)
- Expo 57 for cross-platform APIs

## Code Style & Conventions

### TypeScript
- Always use TypeScript for new code
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use explicit return types for exported functions
- Avoid `any` - use proper typing or `unknown`
- Use strict null checks

### React Native Components
- Use functional components with hooks only
- Prefer named exports over default exports
- Keep components focused and under 300 lines
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks
- Use React.memo for expensive components

### File Naming
- Components: PascalCase (`WellnessScore.tsx`)
- Utilities/hooks: camelCase (`useWellnessScore.ts`)
- Constants: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE in files
- Keep related files together in feature folders

### Imports Order
1. React/React Native imports
2. Third-party libraries
3. Navigation
4. Components
5. Services/utilities
6. Types
7. Styles

### Styling
- Use the theme system from `src/theme/`
- Keep styles at bottom of file with `StyleSheet.create()`
- Use theme colors, spacing, and typography constants
- Consider responsive design for different screen sizes
- Test on both iOS and Android

### State Management
- Use Zustand stores in `src/store/`
- Keep store logic separate from components
- Use selectors to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Persist important state with MMKV

### Firebase
- Always check auth state before Firestore operations
- Use proper security rules (see firestore.rules)
- Handle offline scenarios gracefully
- Use batch operations for multiple writes
- Implement proper error handling

### Navigation
- Use typed navigation with TypeScript
- Keep navigation logic in `src/navigation/`
- Use proper screen options for headers
- Handle deep linking appropriately

## Common Patterns

### API Calls
```typescript
try {
  const result = await someAsyncOperation();
  return result;
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Operation failed:', error);
  // Show user-friendly error
  throw error;
}
```

### Component Structure
```typescript
interface Props {
  // Props interface
}

export const ComponentName: React.FC<Props> = ({ prop1, prop2 }) => {
  // Hooks first
  const [state, setState] = useState();
  const navigation = useNavigation();
  
  // Effects
  useEffect(() => {
    // Effect logic
  }, [dependencies]);
  
  // Handlers
  const handleAction = () => {
    // Handler logic
  };
  
  // Render
  return (
    <View>
      {/* Component JSX */}
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  // Styles
});
```

### Firestore Operations
```typescript
import firestore from '@react-native-firebase/firestore';

// Read
const doc = await firestore()
  .collection('users')
  .doc(userId)
  .get();

// Write
await firestore()
  .collection('users')
  .doc(userId)
  .set(data, { merge: true });
```

## Project Structure

- `src/screens/` - Screen components organized by feature
- `src/components/` - Reusable UI components
- `src/navigation/` - Navigation configuration
- `src/services/` - Business logic (Firebase, health, IAP, etc.)
- `src/store/` - Zustand state stores
- `src/theme/` - Design tokens (colors, spacing, typography)
- `src/config/` - App configuration
- `src/hologram/` - 3D hologram viewer (Android)

## Testing
- Test critical flows (auth, wellness score, care plans)
- Mock Firebase and external services
- Test on both iOS and Android
- Handle edge cases and error states

## Dependencies
- Use `npm install --legacy-peer-deps` for new packages
- Test on both platforms after adding native modules
- Run `cd ios && pod install` after iOS native module changes
- Run `npm run bundle:hologram` after Android hologram changes

## Commits
Use conventional commits:
- `feat:` - New features
- `fix:` - Bug fixes
- `docs:` - Documentation
- `style:` - Code style (formatting)
- `refactor:` - Code refactoring
- `test:` - Adding tests
- `chore:` - Build/tools/dependencies

## Don't
- Don't use default exports
- Don't bypass TypeScript with `any`
- Don't write inline styles (use StyleSheet)
- Don't ignore platform differences
- Don't commit console.logs in production code
- Don't mix business logic with UI components
- Don't store sensitive data in code
- Don't ignore Firebase security rules

## When Making Changes
1. Check existing patterns in codebase
2. Consider both iOS and Android
3. Update types if changing interfaces
4. Test the full user flow
5. Check for console warnings
6. Verify Firebase operations work
7. Update documentation if needed

